## 1 Introduction
Multi-enzymatic cascade reactions, in which two or more natural enzymes catalyze the consecutive reactions in a onepot system without isolation of intermediates, have played key functions in metabolic pathways [1,2] and industrial production [3]. Enzyme catalytic cascade has also obtained significant attention in biosensors [4,5] due to their high catalytic activity and specificity [6,7]. However, the fragile nature of enzymes, including low stability, sensitivity to environmental changes and recycling difficulty, severely restrained the further application of the multi-enzymatic cascade systems.
To address the aforementioned issues, two effective strategies, that is, the immobilization of enzymes in host matrices and the introduction of the nanomaterials possessing enzyme-mimicking activity, have gained considerable attention. The immobilization of enzymes in carriers is one of the most effective methods to improve the stability, activity and recyclability of enzymes [8–10]. Nanozymes, nanomaterials with enzyme-like activities, have become popular artificial enzymes for their structural stability, reusability and adjustable catalytic performance [11–14]. Hence, combining enzyme immobilization and biomimetic catalysis in one pot reaction system provides a desirable alternative way to circumvent those limitations.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are constructed by metal nodes and organic linkers [15–17], have been a class of promising materials to meet the current application needs. On one hand, high surface area, ultrahigh porosity, tunable pore size and good biocompatibility endowed MOFs with great potential in enzyme immobilization [18–21]. On the other hand, tailored chemical functions and abundant metal active sites permitted MOFs to act as excellent nanozymes [19,22,23]. Moreover, the cascade systems composed of natural enzyme and MOFs containing biomimetic activity would integrate the specificity of natural enzymes, and stability and reusability of nanozymes, promoting the advanced applications of Enzyme@MOFs in the biosensing field, including the detection of glucose [22], uric acid [24], cholesterol [25], lactate [26] and acetylcholine [27]. Particularly, MOFs with peroxidase-like activity have attracted immense attention owing to their ability to catalyze oxidation reactions coupled with chromogenic, electrochemical, and fluorescence reactions, realizing quick and convenient target molecule detection in biosensing [27,28].
Although the rapid development of enzyme@MOFs for multi-enzyme catalytic cascade, the size incompatibility of large-sized enzymes and the intrinsic micropores of partial MOFs limited the mass-transportation rates and catalytic efficiency by conventional post-synthetic enzyme immobilization method [29]. Thus, it is necessary to synthesize hierarchically porous MOFs (HP-MOFs), in which the intrinsic micropores ensure the abundant active sites and the size selectivity in catalysis, while the introduced meso/ macropores across the microporous matrix contribute to the enhanced diffusion rates and accessibility to the enzyme active sites, thus permitting higher catalytic activity when applied in enzyme immobilization [10,30–32].
PCN-222 (or named MOF-545 [33]), a Fe-porphyrinbased MOF composed of stable $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}_{6}}$ cluster and heme-like ligands (Fe-TCPP, tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)-porphyrin), possesses ultrahigh stability under extreme conditions and favorable peroxidase-like catalytic activity [34], which provides a promising platform to meet current demands. In addition, the modulator-induced strategy has been successfully applied in the synthesis of hierarchically porous MOFs, including PCN-224 [35] and PCN-250 [36], making a feasible way to synthesize HP-PCN-222 for extensive applications.
Herein, HP-PCN-222(Fe) was successfully synthesized by the modulator-induced method [36,37], in which dodecanoic acid (DA) served as a modulator during the synthetic process and introduced the formation of defects and more mesopores. Then, GOx as a model enzyme, was encapsulated in the HPPCN-222(Fe) by the post-synthetic immobilization method to prepare $\operatorname{GOx}(\mathcal{Q})$ HP-PCN-222(Fe) composite. HP-PCN222(Fe) not only acts as the enzyme immobilization matrix but also executes the function of peroxidase-like activity for glucose detection in a cascade catalytic manner (Scheme 1). The linear range, stability, tolerance, selectivity, and reusability of $\mathrm{GOx}(\varpi\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ were then determined. Finally, a bio-electrochemical sensor for glucose detection was constructed based on GOx@HP-PCN-222(Fe). In principle, the integration of enzyme immobilization and biomimetic catalysis in HP-MOFs expands the potential application for multi-enzymatic cascade reactions.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Synthesis and characterization of GOx@HP-PCN222(Fe)
The hierarchically porous MOF HP-PCN-222(Fe) was successfully synthesized by a modulator-induced defect-formation strategy [37]. The different molar ratio of $\mathrm{DA}/\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ was used to realize the tailorable pore characters of PCN222, acquiring a series of materials of HP-PCN-222(Fe) $(\boldsymbol{X})$ 1 $X=25$ , 50, 75, 100). The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of HP-PCN-222(Fe) (X) and synthesized PCN-222 were similar to that of simulated PCN-222, confirming the crystalline structures of the prepared materials (Figure S1, Supporting Information online). Nitrogen adsorption studies at $77\mathrm{K}$ were performed to evaluate the porosity of HP-PCN222. As shown in Figure S2a, the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption isotherms of PCN-222 and HP-PCN-222(Fe) $(\boldsymbol{X})$ exhibited a type IV curve and a dramatic increase around the point of ${P/{P_{0}}}\mathrm{{=}}0.3$ , a typical character of hierarchically micro-mesoporous structure [34]. The increased $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption and mesoporous distribution of HP-PCN-222(Fe) (X) compared with that in PCN-222 demonstrated the successful introduction of mesopores/porosity modification in HP-PCN-222(Fe) $(\boldsymbol{X})$ . Moreover, the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area was about $1,347\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for HP-PCN-222(Fe) (25), $1,773\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for HP-PCN-222(Fe) (50), $1,780\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for HP-PCN-222 (Fe) (75) and $1,597\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for HP-PCN-222(Fe) (100), repectively. That is, the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption ability of the HP-PCN222(Fe) $(\boldsymbol{X})$ (expect HP-PCN-222(Fe) (100)) enhanced, accompanied by the increased molar ratio of defect modulator (DA) and $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ (Figure S2a). Consistent with these results, the distribution of mesopores centered around $3.25\mathrm{nm}$ was higher in HP-PCN-222(Fe) $(\boldsymbol{X})$ when the $\mathrm{DA}/\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ ratio increased and reached a maximum in HP-PCN-222(Fe) (75) (Figure S2b). The higher distribution of mesopores in HPPCN-222(Fe) $(\boldsymbol{X})$ might be attributed to the defect formation induced by modulator DA. According to the former study, the decreased porosity of mesopores in the materials may be related to the reduction of enzyme-immobilization capacity [35]. Hence, HP-PCN-222(Fe) (75) (abbreviated as HPPCN-222(Fe)) was selected to take as an enzyme-encapsulation matrix.
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM), powder X-ray diffraction and nitrogen adsorption/desorption analysis were performed to investigate the morphology and structure of the samples. SEM images reveal that the normal synthesized PCN-222(Fe) exhibited a rod-like morphology with an average length of around $700\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1a). However, HPPCN-222(Fe) showed a spindle-shaped structure with a rough surface, with an average length of about $860\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1b), due to the introduction of a modulator. After enzyme immobilization, the morphology and particle size of GOx $@$ HP-PCN-222(Fe) (Figure 1c) were in good agreement with that of HP-PCN-222(Fe), which demonstrated that the immobilization process had little effect on the morphology of the matrix. Despite the crystallinity decreasing slightly, the PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{GOx}(\mathcal{D}\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ resembled that of HP-PCN-222(Fe) (Figure 2a). ${\mathrm{GOx}}({\overline{{a}}}{\mathrm{HP}}. $ - PCN-222(Fe) also exhibited a typical type IV nitrogen adsorption/desorption curve as HP-PCN-222(Fe), suggesting the existence of mesopores in materials. Compared with that of HP-PCN-222(Fe) (BET is $1,780\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , the BET surface area $(1,157\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ (Figure S3) and mesopores distribution (Figure 2b) of $\mathrm{GOx}(\varpi\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ decreased simultaneously. These results might be attributed to the successful adsorption of the enzyme into the mesopores during the immobilization. Moreover, the reduced specific surface area might be caused by the binding between water and framework structure during the immobilization process, which slightly decreases the crystallinity, as evidenced by the PXRD patterns.
The samples were also characterized by confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM), $\zeta$ potentials detection, Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR), and thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) to confirm enzyme loading. To directly visualize the GOx immobilization on HP-PCN-222 (Fe), GOx labeled with fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC) was used. The CLSM images revealed that FITC-labeled GOx was immobilized stably on HP-PCN-222(Fe) with clearly green fluorescence (Figure 1d–f). $\zeta$ potential of the samples was detected to explore the electrostatic interaction between enzyme and immobilization matrix. As shown in Figure S4, the $\zeta$ potentials of enzyme and carrier are negative $(-15.6~\mathrm{mV}$ for GOx) and positive ( $47.4\mathrm{mV}$ for PCN-222(Fe) and $71.2\mathrm{mV}$ for HP-PCN-222(Fe)), respectively. The opposite surface charge permits the matrix to immobilize the enzyme with an electrostatic interaction. After immobilization, the $\zeta$ potentials of $\operatorname{GOx}\varpi\operatorname{PCN-}222(\operatorname{Fe})$ and GOx@HP-PCN-222(Fe) changed to 43.4 and $27.2~\mathrm{mV},$ respectively, confirming the immobilization of enzyme on the surface of the matrix under electrostatic interaction. The lower $\zeta$ potential of ${\mathrm{GOx}}({\mathcal{Q}}{\mathrm{HP}}.$ PCN-222(Fe) than that of GOx@PCN-222(Fe) may be attributed to more immobilized GOx in HP-PCN-222(Fe).
FTIR analysis was performed to further confirm GOx immobilization. The FTIR spectra of GOx@HP-PCN-222 (Fe) displayed a new absorption peak from $^{\sim1,575}$ to 1,480 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , attributed to the bending vibration of –NH– and $-C N-$ , corresponding to amide II [38] (Figure 2c), which were not observed in the HP-PCN-222(Fe) spectrum. The peak at around $1,635\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , belonging to the carbonyl vibration peak in $\operatorname{GOx}$ , coincided with symmetric stretching of carbonyl group at around $1{,}601\ \mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in HP-PCN-222(Fe) and $\mathrm{GOx}(\mathcal{D}\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ . Besides, stretching/bending vibration of the Fe–N bond around $1{,}000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was observed in HP-PCN-222(Fe) before and after GOx immobilization (Figure 2c), indicating potential peroxidase-like catalytic activities of this material. Further, we also explored the specific interactions between GOx and the PCN-222(Fe)/ HP-PCN-222(Fe) host by Raman spectroscopy. The Raman spectra of samples were determined according to the aforementioned method [39]. The samples were scanned by an excitation laser at $532\mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in Figure S6a, the Raman spectrum of $\mathrm{GOx}(\mathcal{D}\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ exhibited both the characteristic bands of GOx and PCN-222(Fe), indicating the presence of GOx in PCN-222(Fe). Compared with the characteristic peaks in GOx and HP-PCN-222(Fe), there are some energy shifts of the Raman bands found in ${\mathrm{GOx}}({\overline{{a}}}{\mathrm{HP}}. $ - PCN-222(Fe) composite. For example, the characteristic peaks of GOx at $861~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (CNC symmetric stretching of amine group) and $1{,}349\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (amide III) [40,41] were blueshifted to 869 and $1{,}353\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively, for ${\mathrm{GOx}}({\overline{{a}}}{\mathrm{HP}}. $ - PCN-222(Fe). According to literature, the characteristic peaks of iron porphyrin analyzed by Raman spectra in PCN222(Fe) could be classified into several types, including the spin-state sensitive band C $(1,545-1,498~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ and D $(1,565-1,540\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ), the oxidation state marker band E (391– $376\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\cdot$ ), and both the oxidation and spin state sensitive band A $(1,370-1,345\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ [42]. Interestingly, the spin-state sensitive band at around 1,499 and $1{,}555\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for HP-PCN222(Fe) [42] were red-shifted to 1,497 and $1,553\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively, for $\operatorname{GOx}\textcircled{a}\operatorname{PCN-}222(\operatorname{Fe})$ . Similar FTIR (Figure S5) and Raman spectroscopy (Figure S6b) results were also observed in GOx@PCN-222(Fe). These results indicated the spin state or oxidation state of iron porphyrin environments in MOF matrix (PCN-222(Fe) and HP-PCN-222(Fe) was changed by the possible interaction between GOx and the MOF matrix after enzyme immobilization.
TGA analysis was performed to further confirmed the presence of GOx in GOx@HP-PCN-222(Fe). Compared with that of HP-PCN-222(Fe), the weight of ${\mathrm{GOx}}({\overline{{a}}}{\mathrm{HP}}. $ - PCN-222(Fe) decreased sharply in the range of 230 and 500 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , ascribed to GOx decomposition (Figure 2d). As a result of the weight loss values, the GOx content in ${\mathrm{GOx}}({\overline{{a}}}{\mathrm{HP}}. $ - PCN-222(Fe) composite was calculated as about $7.5\%$ , similar to that $(7\%)$ determined by BCA assay. These findings together suggested the successful immobilization of GOx on HP-PCN-222(Fe). Besides, the GOx content determined by BCA assay in $\operatorname{GOx}\textcircled{\omega}\operatorname{PCN-}222(\operatorname{Fe})$ was about $5.5\%$ , lower than that in $\mathrm{GOx}(\mathcal{D}\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ composite. These results might confirm the former hypothesis, that is, HP-PCN$222(\mathrm{Fe})$ possessed higher $\zeta$ potentials than PCN-222(Fe), enabling stronger electrostatic interaction to attract more GOx for preparing immobilized enzyme.
## 2.2 Cascade reaction
HP-PCN-222(Fe) possessed the potential to serve as an immobilization matrix for its hierarchically micro-mesoporous structure and wonderful peroxidase-like catalytic activity for its iron porphyrin-based ligands [34,43], which allowed glucose detection combined with glucose oxidase (GOx). GOx could catalyze the oxidization of glucose with $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , generating gluconic acid and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Then, HP-PCN-222(Fe) could catalyze chromogenic substrate, that is TMB, accompanied by the generated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce oxTMB (characteristic peak at $450\mathrm{nm}$ after addition of sulfuric acid), indicating the catalytic capability of the composite (Figure 3a). Then, the performance of the integrated enzyme on glucose detection cascade reaction was detected. As shown in Figure 3b, GOx@HP-PCN-222(Fe)/Glucose/TMB system could catalyze the cascade reaction and showed remarkable changes with a characteristic peak at $450\mathrm{nm}$ . However, there is no obvious color change in the reaction system in the absence of one component of the reaction system mentioned above. As expected, the color intensity and the absorbance at $450\mathrm{nm}$ of solution catalyzed by $\operatorname{GOx}\ @\operatorname{HP-PCN}.$ -222(Fe) gradually increased as the concentration of glucose rose (Figure 3c). These results indicated that GOx@HP-PCN-222 (Fe) was useful for glucose detection. The optimum $\mathsf{p H}$ and temperature of the cascade reaction catalyzed by ${\mathrm{GOx}}({\mathcal{Q}}{\mathrm{HP}}. $ PCN-222(Fe) were about 4.0 and $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S7), respectively.
Even though both the free enzyme (combined with ligand Fe-TCPPCl) and integrated enzyme (GOx@HP-PCN-222 (Fe) and $\operatorname{GOx}\textcircled{\omega}\operatorname{PCN-}222(\operatorname{Fe}))$ could catalyze the cascade reaction, $\mathrm{GOx}(\mathcal{D}\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ exhibited superior catalytic activity compared with that of the free enzyme (Figure S8b) and $\operatorname{GOx}\textcircled{\omega}\operatorname{PCN-}222(\operatorname{Fe})$ (Figure 4). Particularly, the absorbance at the characteristic peak of the reaction solution catalyzed by $\mathrm{GOx}(\mathcal{D}\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ displayed a good linear relationship with the glucose concentration in the range of 0 to $200~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , attaining a limit of detection (LOD) of $0.237\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (Figure 4). However, the detection limit in similar biosensors composed of GOx@PCN-222(Fe) was $2.50\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . Although both the PCN-222(Fe) and HP-PCN-222(Fe) exhibited peroxidase-like activity, the developed biosensor composed of GOx@HP-PCN-222(Fe) composite exhibited a wider linear range, higher sensitivity and lower LOD compared to that of GOx@PCN-222(Fe), which might be related to the better mass transport efficiency and enzyme accessibility ascribed to the hierarchically micro-mesoporous structure in HP-PCN-222(Fe). Thus, GOx@HP-PCN-222 (Fe) displayed superior catalysis and sensing performance.
The PXRD pattern of the GOx@HP-PCN-222(Fe) after biocatalysis was similar to that of the one without participating in biocatalysis and the simulated PCN-222(Fe), confirming that the crystalline structure of GOx@HP-PCN-222 (Fe) remained relatively intact after biocatalysis reaction (Figure 2a). Thus, HP-PCN-222(Fe) can be used as not only an enzyme immobilization matrix but also a peroxidase-like enzyme, permitting $\mathrm{GOx}\textcircled{\omega}\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ to be applied for the detection of glucose.
The stability of the immobilized enzyme in an extreme environment is really important for practical application. Thus, we have examined the catalytic activity of ${\mathrm{GOx}}({\overline{{a}}}{\mathrm{HP}}. $ - PCN-222(Fe) in heat and acid/alkali treatment.
Firstly, $\mathrm{GOx}(\varpi\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ and free GOx were incubated in DI water from 30 to $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ heat treatment at a specific temperature, $\mathrm{GOx}(\varpi\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ showed higher catalytic activity than free $\operatorname{GOx}$ (Figure S9a). To further verify the thermal stability, we measured the activity of the integrated and free enzyme that incubated at $60^{\circ}$ C for $12\mathrm{h}$ . The relative activity of $\mathrm{GOx}(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ remained at approximately $50\%$ of its initial activity after treatment at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . In contrast, a free enzyme in the same condition lost nearly $74\%$ of the overall activity after
$12\mathrm{h}$ (Figure 5a). The higher residual activity in ${\mathrm{GOx}}({\overline{{a}}}{\mathrm{HP}}. $ - PCN-222(Fe) compared with free form may be due to the protection of HP-PCN-222(Fe) on the enzyme, thus enhancing the thermal stability of GOx. In order to test the acidbase tolerance, the integrated and free enzyme was incubated in sodium acetate buffer solution that was acidic (pH 4) or alkaline $\mathrm{(pH~10)}$ solution except in neutral condition $\left(\mathrm{pH}7\right)$ . In the neutral condition, the $\mathrm{GOx}(\varpi\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ showed a minimal drop in the catalytic activity over $20\mathrm{d}$ (about $14\%$ , while GOx lost about $32.5\%$ of its initial activity (Figure S9b). More significant differences in residual activity of the immobilized enzyme and free enzyme were observed in acidic and alkaline conditions. The ${\mathrm{GOx}}({\mathcal{Q}}{\mathrm{HP}}. $ PCN-222(Fe) maintained $57\%$ and $81\%$ catalytic activity of its initial activity after being incubated in acidic $\mathrm{(pH~4)}$ and alkaline $\mathrm{(pH~10)}$ conditions over $20\mathrm{d}$ , which is 2-fold and 1.5-fold higher than that of free enzyme in the same condition, respectively (Figure S9c, d). Thus, the immobilized enzyme exhibited higher acid-base tolerance. Furthermore, $\mathrm{GOx}(\varpi\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ composite displayed negligible leakage over $20\mathrm{d}$ incubation (Figure 5b), proving that $\mathrm{GOx}(\mathcal{D}\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ possesses good storage stability.
The specificity is a crucial element in estimating the feasibility of $\mathrm{GOx}(\varpi\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ as a glucose detector. Various interfering compounds, including bovine serum albumin (BSA, $0.15\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ ), sucrose $(0.1\ \mathrm{mM})$ , lactose (0.1 mM), fructose $(0.1\ \mathrm{mM})$ , and maltose $(0.1\mathrm{mM})$ were introduced into the reaction system, and negligible responses of the solutions were observed compared with glucose (Figure 5c), conforming that the outstanding anti-interference capacity of GOx@HP-PCN-222(Fe) toward glucose detection.
Reusability is another important requirement for industrial application and also a major contribution to enzyme encapsulation in MOFs. In recycling tests, GOx@HP-PCN-222 (Fe) remained about $96\%$ of its original catalytic activity after five cycles, while free GOx nearly lost all of its activity after two catalytic cycles, indicating the excellent reusability of the encapsulated enzyme (Figure 5d). To sum, ${\mathrm{GOx}}({\mathcal{Q}}{\mathrm{HP}}. $ PCN-222(Fe) possessed storage stability, acid-alkali resistance, specificity toward glucose detection and reusability, which is promising for practical application.
## 2.3 Electrochemical detection
Bio-electrochemical detection is a practical and effective method for biosensors [44]. Thus, we investigated the electrocatalytic activity of GOx@HP-PCN-222(Fe) toward glucose detection. Graphene was widely applied in biosensor construction for its excellent electrical conductivity [45]. Herein, $\mathrm{GOx}(\mathcal{D}\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ was mixed with high conductive graphene and nafion to fabricate the glassy carbon electrodes (GCE) for the construction of GOx@HP-PCN222(Fe)/Graphene/Nafion/GCE according to the literature [46]. The fabricated bioelectrode was characterized by cyclic voltammograms (CVs) at the potential range from $+0.4$ to $-1.0\mathrm{V}.$ The cathodic peak current at about $-0.55\mathrm{V}$ enlarged when the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration rose from 1 to $80\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , indicating the electrocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{GOx}\textcircled{\omega}\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222$ (Fe) towards the reduction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ due to the presence of HP-PCN-222(Fe) (Figure 6a). Moreover, the cathodic peak current increased as glucose concentration increased from 0 to $25~\mathrm{mM}$ (Figure 6b). With the addition of glucose, $\mathrm{GOx}(\mathcal{D}\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{G}$ raphene/Nafion/GCE would catalyze the oxidation of glucose and the reduction of oxygen, resulting in the formation of gluconic acid and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , respectively. Next, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ would be reduced further by HP
PCN-222(Fe) in the modified electrode. Thus, the cathodic peak current is related to the reduction of oxygen and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , reflecting the glucose signal indirectly. Because the oxidization of glucose was an aerobic reaction, the catalytic performance of the biosensor in $\mathrm{N}_{2^{-}}$ , air- and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated PBS was also explored. As expected, the biosensor in $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated PBS exhibited a more prominent response to the glucose compared with other gas atmospheres (Figure 6c and Figure S10b–d). In addition, the cathodic peak currents increased with the increasing scan rates accordingly and moved to more negative values as the scan rates increased from 20 to $100\mathrm{mVs}^{-1}$ , indicating a positive correlation with scan rates (Figure 6d and Figure S10a). These results indicated that $\mathrm{GOx}(\varpi\mathrm{HP-PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})$ is an available bioelectrochemical sensor for glucose detection.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, we report a facile method to realize favorable multienzymatic cascade reactions by combining natural enzyme immobilization and biomimetic catalysis based on the porphyrin-containing zirconium HP-MOFs. HP-PCN-222 (Fe) with different mesopores distribution was synthesized by a modulator-induced-defect formation strategy. HP-PCN222(Fe) acted as not only a matrix for GOx encapsulation but also a peroxidase-like nanoenzyme, which allowed higher cascade reaction efficiency for glucose detection. Compared with GOx@PCN-222(Fe) and free enzyme, GOx@HP-PCN222(Fe) composite applied in glucose detection exhibited higher catalytic activity, stability, acid-base tolerance, selectivity and recyclability. Moreover, the electrode fabricated by GOx@HP-PCN-222(Fe) showed favorable electrochemical performance toward glucose detection. This versatile synthetic strategy paves the way for the preparation of diverse stable E@HP-MOFs composite by integrating the advantages of enzyme immobilization and biomimetic catalysis for extending the potential application of multienzymatic cascade reaction systems.
